<p>
  <strong>Life emerged.</strong> <%= link_to "Run 41", run_path(41) %> is the clearest
  case: <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> sits near 0.94 for five thousand epochs,
  falls off a cliff at epoch 5 030 and ends at 0.26, its
  <span class="mono">op_density</span> rises from the random soup's 0.10 to a peak near
  0.15, and its
  snapshots show one colony spreading across the torus from that epoch on. The five
  transitioned runs end between 0.05 and 0.26; every one of the 95 censored runs ends
  above 0.90. That is the BFF result, reproduced here with nothing selecting for it.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The window is not there.</strong> Emergence happened at
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-13</sup></span>, <span class="mono">2<sup>-12</sup></span>,
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-9</sup></span> and <span class="mono">2<sup>-8</sup></span>
  alike — one seed in ten, two at best, wherever it happened, with no peak in between and
  no sign of an upper error threshold anywhere in the range swept. At ten seeds per arm
  the gap between 2/10 and 0/10 is inside what the sweep can resolve, so the arms at or
  above <span class="mono">2<sup>-13</sup></span> are not distinguishable from each other.
  The hypothesis of DESIGN §1.3 sweep 1, as stated, is not supported by its own data.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>A lower cutoff, suggested and not established.</strong> The one pattern that
  survives pooling is that nothing emerged below
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-13</sup></span>: 0 of 40 runs at rates
  ≤ <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> against 5 of 60 at
  ≥ <span class="mono">2<sup>-13</sup></span>. A one-sided Fisher exact test on that 2×2
  table gives <span class="mono">p ≈ 0.073</span>. That is suggestive and nothing more —
  and the split was drawn after looking at where the transitions fell, which makes it
  weaker than the number looks.
</p>

<p>
  What the mutation rate does change, and changes unambiguously, is the floor a soup
  without replicators sits on: among the censored runs the final
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> climbs arm by arm from ≈ 0.92 at
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> to ≈ 0.99 at
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-8</sup></span> without a single reversal. Below
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> there is no such order: those three arms sit
  between 0.91 and 0.93, with the no-mutation arm the highest of them. That is mutation
  randomising the tapes faster than interaction can make them alike. It is real, it is
  large, and it has nothing to do with emergence.
</p>

<p>
  Two caveats survive from the interim reading. The
  <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> detector fires in only three of the five
  transitioned runs — it peaks at 867 cells in run 41, 108 in run 45, 14 in run 83, and
  never leaves zero in runs 59 and 95 — and it ends at zero in all five, so what the
  transition detects is the compression-and-diversity signature rather than a standing
  census of copiers. It also fired briefly, on 2 cells, in one censored run at
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-8</sup></span>. And nothing here is a clean negative until the
  <%= link_to "positive control", finding_path("bff-control") %> transitions: a sweep with
  five events in a hundred runs is a weak instrument whichever way it points.
</p>

<h2>What next</h2>
<p>
  Emergence at 128×128 within 20 000 epochs is rare enough that this sweep mostly measured
  censoring: 95 of 100 runs stopped before anything happened, so most of what was compared
  between arms is where the clock ran out. Turning "one seed in ten" into a rate needs
  longer runs, larger worlds, or both, and the sweeps for that are queued.
  <%= link_to "Mutation rate, long runs", experiment_path("mutation-rate-long") %> re-runs
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span>…<span class="mono">2<sup>-11</sup></span> for
  60 000 epochs — three times the budget — to separate "rare" from "slow" and to test the
  lower cutoff exactly where it was drawn.
  <%= link_to "World size", experiment_path("world-size") %> asks whether emergence is a
  per-world event or a per-cell rate; if it is per-cell, a bigger world is the cheapest way
  to buy events. And the
  <%= link_to "BFF positive control", experiment_path("bff-control") %> — 2<sup>17</sup>
  tapes, well-mixed, 50 000 epochs — is the run that says whether this engine reaches the
  published emergence rate at all. Until it finishes, every censored run above is evidence
  about the instrument as much as about the soup.
</p>
